---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url.js';
import { CONTACT, DONATE, COSTS, money } from '../lib/site.js';
const elements = await getCollection('elements');
const tests = await getCollection('tests');
---
<Base title="About" subtitle="Who made this, how it is built, what it costs and what it is not.">
  <h1>About the guide</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A field guide to figure skating, written for skaters, parents and anyone who has
    been handed a syllabus and told to go and learn a bracket.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it exists</h2>
  <p>
    Most of what a skater needs is either locked inside a coach's head or scattered
    across videos filmed at the far end of a rink. Neither is much use at seven in
    the morning when you are trying to remember which way the free hip is supposed
    to face. So this is the other thing: a page per element, the same three views
    every time, and a plain statement of which test it belongs to.
  </p>
  <p>
    It covers {elements.length} elements across {tests.length} tests so far, and it
    will keep growing — edges and turns first, then spins, then dance and the
    competition-level material, kept on their own tabs so that a skater on their
    first test is not confronted with a triple.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is built</h2>
  <p>
    Almost nothing here is drawn or written one element at a time. Which way a lobe
    curves follows from three facts about the foot — which foot, which edge, forwards
    or backwards — so the guide records those three and derives the rest. The figures
    are geometry rendered from a body-frame rig, not clip art, which is why the same
    move can be shown from above, from the side and from behind without anyone
    redrawing it, and why every element exists for both feet.
  </p>
  <p>
    The consequence a reader notices is that the guide mirrors for free. A skater who
    rotates the other way gets the same detail as everyone else, rather than being
    told to hold a diagram up to a mirror.
  </p>

  <h2>What it is not</h2>
  <p>
    It is not affiliated with British Ice Skating, with the ISU, or with any club or
    rink. Where it uses their names for things it does so because those are the names
    a British skater will be examined on. No governing body's material is reproduced:
    every word and every figure here is written and drawn from scratch, from public
    sources, and the syllabus documents themselves are read locally and never
    redistributed.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is also not a substitute for a coach. Nobody has ever learned a rocker off a
    web page. What this can do is tell you what you are trying to do before you get
    on the ice, and give you something to look at afterwards.
  </p>

  <h2>Is it correct?</h2>
  <p>
    Not yet verified, and the guide says so on every page rather than hoping you do
    not ask. The mechanics are derived from first principles and cross-checked
    against the published syllabuses, which catches a great deal and cannot catch
    everything. The plan is to have the descriptions checked by people who teach
    this for a living: a passage carries a tick once several of them have signed it
    off, and a single objection pulls that tick straight back down.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you coach, judge or officiate — currently, or you used to —{' '}
    <a href={url('coaches/')}>please get in touch</a>. If you do not, and you have
    spotted something wrong, that is just as welcome: write to{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What it costs</h2>
  <div class="derived">
    <p>
      Hosting is free — the site is static files on GitHub Pages. The only real bill
      is the domain: {money(COSTS.domain)} a year to register, plus{' '}
      {money(COSTS.privacy)} for privacy, so {money(COSTS.total)} a year all in.
      That is the whole of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      There will never be a subscription, an account wall or an advert, because the
      point is that a skater who cannot afford another lesson can still read it.{' '}
      {DONATE
        ? <>If you can afford to, <a href={DONATE}>buying a coffee</a> covers the domain
          and the evenings. If you cannot, read it anyway — that is what it is for.</>
        : <>A donation link will appear here shortly, for anyone who would like to
          chip in towards the domain. Until then there is nothing to pay and nothing
          to click.</>}
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Who made it</h2>
  <p>
    One person, in Surrey, in the evenings, with a great deal of help from a skater
    who kept pointing out that the free foot was in the wrong place. Corrections,
    arguments and better wording all to{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
